#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055780} :activity/id #uuid "a6abcd14-76de-59df-adaa-1dc632470883" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986 :topic/id #uuid "2424a53c-3dea-5c8f-aeb6-365cf563d5f2" :topic/math-academy-id 38} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056020 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\nx + 4 < 7\n$$\n\nTo **solve** this inequality, we need to isolate the variable $x$ on one side.\n\nInequalities are just like equations because they can be solved using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nSo, to solve this inequality, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & <7 \\\\ x+4-4 & <7-4 \\\\ x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $x < 3.$ This tells us that any value of $x$ smaller than $3$ satisfies the original inequality.\n\nWe can represent the solution to this inequality using a number line as follows:\n\n![](images/55/556401d043fbd285a42b257e3c32f0a858377355674c264b90396e43ca8a8a13.png)\n\nLet's take a look at an inequality that we can solve using the multiplication principle." :tutorial/id #uuid "63426fbc-7f6c-516c-a3e9-9f1262862928" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8099 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5d1cc5da-4e34-51ae-843a-e59df07e8c64" :step/math-academy-id 52055 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055781}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055781 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054399 :question/id #uuid "121c55cc-404a-53e7-81f7-6edba34b0796" :question/math-academy-id "e-12956"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf8a6a4f-38ce-5ece-bdcb-6f2b58e2605c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489254}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a One-Step Inequality"} :step/id #uuid "9e6e8c20-c9b3-5288-8894-994f1e8693a4" :step/math-academy-id 52056 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056021 :tutorial/content "We've seen that solving linear inequalities is similar to solving linear equations.\n\nHowever, there is one additional rule that we need to remember when solving inequalities:\n\n*When multiplying or dividing both sides by a **negative number**, we flip the inequality sign.*\n\nWhen we \"flip the inequality sign,\" we switch the direction of the inequality symbol. For example:\n\n- \"$\\color{blue}<$\" becomes \"$\\color{red}>$\" and vice versa\n\n- \"$\\color{blue}\\leq$\" becomes \"$\\color{red}\\geq$\" and vice versa\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n- 3x \\gt 9\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality using the multiplication principle. However, when we divide both sides by $-3,$ we have to flip the inequality sign. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x\\,\\, & >\\,\\,9 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{9}{-3} \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-3 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-3}x}{\\cancel{-3}}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-3 \\\\ x\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\lt -3.$\n\nWe'll discuss why we need to flip the inequality sign at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying this idea." :tutorial/id #uuid "2c47a872-da72-51b3-ac92-ef955acc3f08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8100 :tutorial/title "Multiplying or Dividing an Inequality by a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "cb7661d0-6da1-518d-9228-3db6e6b0cead" :step/math-academy-id 52059 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055783}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054400 :question/id #uuid "7424a287-2269-5044-abb7-1004b3826aca" :question/math-academy-id "e-12957"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "281841fb-287e-597a-abb1-3917212e0dcb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507412}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a One-Step Inequality When Flipping Is Required"} :step/id #uuid "18957d57-5792-5701-b6f4-614dc92db70f" :step/math-academy-id 52060 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054401 :question/id #uuid "82a9a75f-ab0e-54c3-9a0d-82c8497714cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12958"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "200fc394-b299-5114-8f39-e6265f3c1166" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489197}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a One-Step Inequality With Fractions When Flipping Is Required"} :step/id #uuid "0607f740-0035-5986-a51a-77aa35a3ca25" :step/math-academy-id 52063 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055785}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054402 :question/id #uuid "1154ded3-0841-5358-980c-15c0f1841e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-12959"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b0cad0-354b-5c4c-9fe2-3aceff7e5b6e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507499}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality and Adjusting the Final Result"} :step/id #uuid "351bf5fa-de4c-5dd3-b946-23f3767c42b1" :step/math-academy-id 52066 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056022 :tutorial/content "We'll now learn why we must flip the inequality sign when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number. \n\nTo help us, let's consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n6 > 0\n$$\n\n \n\nThis is a true statement, and it remains true if we multiply or divide both sides by a positive number. \n\nFor example:\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $2$ gives $12 > 0,$ which is *true*.$\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- Dividing the inequality by $2$ gives $3 > 0,$ which is also *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nHowever, if we multiply or divide both sides by a negative number, *without* flipping the inequality sign, we get a false statement.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $-2$ *without flipping the inequality sign* gives $-12 > 0,$ which is *false*. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\n- Dividing the inequality by $-2$ *without flipping the inequality sign* gives $-3 > 0,$ which is also *false*. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo, whenever we multiply or divide by a negative number, we need to flip the sign of the inequality for it to remain true.\n\n- Multiplying the inequality by $-2$ *and flipping the inequality sign* gives $-12 < 0,$ which is *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- Dividing the inequality by $-2$ *and flipping the sign* gives $-3 < 0,$ which is also *true*. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f9eacf1c-4f75-5dfe-a8e7-798404a6280d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8101 :tutorial/title "Why We Flip the Inequality Sign"} :step/id #uuid "e300a5cf-0bdb-5c23-9a03-3bb60a12a788" :step/math-academy-id 52070}] :activity/title "Solving One-Step Linear Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054900}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507531} :activity/id #uuid "f76e562d-eefb-5f49-ba13-fb8a997a1b70" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986 :topic/id #uuid "2424a53c-3dea-5c8f-aeb6-365cf563d5f2" :topic/math-academy-id 38} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507412 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3dff68-6795-523f-999b-07af4af3ec70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506706} {:answer/id #uuid "7668d459-4f42-5919-bb7f-fa7eec09053a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y>9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506946} {:answer/id #uuid "9074c12a-1d5b-5242-a19c-1b503006d445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y<9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506999} {:answer/id #uuid "acb62ac6-c48e-569b-a36c-c8b94883aa98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y>-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507060} {:answer/id #uuid "e10be7fe-3362-55cd-9dae-30e59a1d1e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y<-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507168} :answer-field/id #uuid "1301f956-a5f9-5af2-b9fc-78b855c39882" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2d8f9e5d-ffea-59b8-9f7c-2f9037766a19" :question/math-academy-id "q-6277" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-2y>18.$" :question/worked-solution "The variable $y$ is being *multiplied* by $-2.$ To isolate $y,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-2.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y\\,\\, & >\\,\\,18 \\\\ \\frac{-2y}{-2}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{18}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{-2y}{-2}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-2$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2y}{-2} & <-9 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}y}{\\cancel{-2}} & <-9 \\\\ y & <-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y<-9.$"} :step/id #uuid "015a9fb1-4671-5e91-a484-e36a765ef7c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ddfe654-6c20-5256-8c77-e26f467b5752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506823} :answer-field/id #uuid "844948db-6db2-5198-81c4-adc418e774dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507318} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ff7601c-194b-518f-be31-b3982ec9d2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506935} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfff5c34-1b4e-574b-b2cd-51c419fcd650" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1670dd77-8590-5cdd-be80-9cfcce9e381b" :question/math-academy-id "q-181002" :question/problem "The solution to the inequality $-9a\\le -54$ is $a${{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The variable $a$ is being *multiplied* by $-9.$ To isolate $a,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-9.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9a\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-54 \\\\ \\frac{-9a}{-9}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-54}{-9} \\\\ \\frac{-9a}{-9}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-9$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-9a}{-9} & \\ge 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-9}a}{\\cancel{-9}} & \\ge 6 \\\\ a & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a\\ge 6.$"} :step/id #uuid "40b41b12-df46-55fe-92ac-57937129a72d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "825a3f94-5d5f-59b8-9d4e-7d0f168885a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z>-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506965} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfb137d-cdc8-5e43-84c6-ea0025793bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z<24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507016} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a10480-cbf4-58a2-9e6b-17815d58cadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z<-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507026} {:answer/id #uuid "b207b853-0fed-5188-a900-3bb18b72d272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z>\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507070} {:answer/id #uuid "ef835f55-6239-5e48-941a-ae73fc43925e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z<-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4490670-0ae0-5d23-9e69-8c96fae8a13a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c8a935c4-ddf1-51df-a83c-2659ff68e2ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-154041" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4<-\\frac{z}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The variable $z$ is being *divided* by $6.$ To isolate $z$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-6$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-\\frac{z}{6} \\\\ (-6)\\cdot 4\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\,\\, & >(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >(\\cancel{-}6)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{6z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{\\cancel{6}z}{\\cancel{6}} \\\\ -24 & >z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nz<-24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z<-24.$"} :step/id #uuid "86fe7023-fab7-5333-bca0-7bf71a617112" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507546}}] :activity/title "Solving One-Step Linear Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506677}]}
